@meta
id: 22-06
title: Grow the selection a level
chapter: 22
type: lesson
skills: visual-char
par: 33
minutes: 5
vscode: Shift+Alt+Right, Shift+Alt+Right, Shift+Alt+Right, type
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
`ci(` changes what is inside the nearest brackets. When the cursor is
inside a call that is inside another call, "nearest" means the inner
one, and to reach the outer one you have to move the cursor out first.

In visual mode a text object can be pressed again. `vi(` selects inside
the innermost brackets; a second `i(` grows the selection to include
those brackets; a third grows it to the inside of the next pair out.
Each press is one level. You watch it grow and stop when it is right.

The payment service's `refund` now takes the whole order. Change the
call in `cancel` to:

    return this.paymentService.refund(order);

The cursor starts in the middle of the inner `Math.round(...)` call —
that is where a search for `total` would leave you — and the whole
argument list has to go.

@keys
| `vi(` | select inside the nearest brackets |
| `i(` again | grow the selection one level outward |
| `a(` | grow it to include the brackets |
| `c` | change the selection |

@hints
Search for `total *` to land inside the inner call. `vi(` lights up
`order.total * 100`. That is one level too deep.
--
Press `i(` again. Now the brackets are included. Once more, and the
selection is everything inside `refund(...)`.
--
`/total \*` `Enter`, `vi(i(i(`, then `c`, type `order`, `Esc`.

@solution
    /total \*<Enter> v i ( i ( i ( c order <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes, and nothing to count or to move. This is the one
thing text objects do in visual mode that they cannot do after an
operator: repeat. `ci(` commits to a level; `vi(` lets you climb. The
same works for `i{` in nested blocks, `it` in nested tags, and `ip`
when one paragraph is not enough.

`a(` grows the same way but includes the brackets at each step. The
first `a(` gives `(order.total * 100)`; the second gives the whole
`(order.id, Math.round(...))`. Use whichever leaves you with exactly
the region you want to replace.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, Math.round(order.total * 100));
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order);
  }
}
